      "title": "Ethiopia Elevates Sea Access to Existential Security Issue at Tana Forum - Birr Metrics",
      "link": "https://birrmetrics.com/ethiopia-elevates-sea-access-to-existential-security/",
      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:44:59 GMT",
      "image": "https://birrmetrics.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/858345545_2684950075275650_63321.jpg",
      "content": "Ethiopia Elevates Sea Access to Existential Security Issue Skip to content October, 2026 Birr Metrics News Feature Insights Interview Startups Editorial Digital Magazine Search for: Search Search Birr Metrics News Feature Insights Interview Startups Editorial Digital Magazine Ethiopia Elevates Sea Access to Existential Security Issue at Tana Forum Amanuel Kindeya October 24, 2026 Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed links Ethiopia’s dependence on foreign ports to national sovereignty, regional security and trade competitiveness as African leaders debate economic independence and geopolitical transformation. Ethiopia has renewed its diplomatic push for access to the sea, with Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed (PhD) describing maritime access as an existential issue affecting the country’s long-term security, economic sovereignty and ability to participate in international trade. Addressing the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa in Bahir Dar on October 24, 2026, Abiy placed Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions within a wider continental discussion about economic independence, regional integration and Africa’s position in a changing geopolitical order. The Prime Minister said Ethiopia, a country of more than 130 million people, faces long-term strategic and economic constraints because its international trade routes pass through neighbouring countries. He maintained that Ethiopia would continue pursuing maritime access through dialogue and arrangements based on mutual benefit, presenting the issue as both a national security priority and an economic development concern. The remarks come as Ethiopia seeks to diversify its external trade routes, reduce dependence on the Djibouti corridor and negotiate more reliable access to international shipping networks. Ethiopia has been landlocked since Eritrea gained independence in 1993, ending its direct access to the Red Sea through Assab and Massawa. The country subsequently developed a heavy dependence on Djibouti, which now handles more than 90 percent of its international merchandise trade. Djibouti’s port infrastructure, connected to Ethiopia by road and the Addis Ababa–Djibouti railway, serves as the country’s principal international trade gateway. The corridor supports imports of petroleum, machinery, industrial inputs and consumer goods, as well as exports of coffee, manufactured products and other commodities. This concentration means disruptions involving port operations, transport infrastructure, border procedures or regional security can affect Ethiopian importers, exporters and domestic supply chains. The financial implications extend beyond port charges. Importers and exporters incur costs associated with inland transportation, customs clearance, container handling, storage and cargo delivery between Djibouti and inland logistics facilities such as Modjo. The government has repeatedly identified high logistics costs and lengthy cargo-processing procedures as constraints on Ethiopia’s manufacturing competitiveness and export development. The maritime question has also become a major diplomatic issue in the Horn of Africa. In January 2024, Ethiopia signed a memorandum of understanding with Somaliland concerning access to the coast. The agreement triggered opposition from Somalia, which considers Somaliland part of its sovereign territory, and led to a diplomatic dispute between Addis Ababa and Mogadishu. The two countries subsequently signed the Ankara Declaration on December 11, 2024, following mediation by Türkiye. Under the declaration, Ethiopia and Somalia reaffirmed respect for each other’s sovereignty, unity and territorial integrity while acknowledging the potential economic benefits of Ethiopia obtaining reliable access to the sea. The agreement envisaged mutually beneficial commercial arrangements, including contracts and leases, that would provide Ethiopia with secure and sustainable maritime access under Somalia’s sovereign authority. The declaration established a diplomatic framework for negotiations but did not itself grant Ethiopia a port or sovereign maritime territory. Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions also intersect with wider competition in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden, where strategic shipping routes connect the Indian Ocean to the Mediterranean through the Suez Canal. Djibouti hosts foreign military facilities operated by several countries, including the United States, China, France and Japan. The wider maritime region has also faced security disruptions affecting international shipping, increasing attention to the reliability of trade routes. Against this background, Abiy linked Ethiopia’s position to the broader African challenge of securing predictable access to markets. He said transport corridors and more efficient cross-border arrangements were essential to enabling African producers to reach regional and international buyers. The issue is closely connected to the African Continental Free Trade Area, which aims to expand trade among African economies through tariff reductions, improved market access and measures addressing non-tariff barriers. For landlocked economies, implementing continental trade agreements also depends on transport infrastructure, customs cooperation and access to ports in neighbouring countries. The 12th Tana Forum, held from October 23 to 25 under the theme “Pathways to Peace in Africa: Economic Sovereignty and Geopolitical Transformation,” brings together African leaders, policymakers, researchers and representatives of regional institutions. Its agenda examines how control over economic resources, financial systems, trade networks and strategic infrastructure affects national sovereignty and the continent’s capacity to maintain peace. Abiy also presented Ethiopia’s domestic development and governance philosophy, Medemer, meaning synergy, as part of his wider argument for cooperation and collective African action. The Prime Minister has developed the concept through a series of publications addressing national unity, cooperation and economic transformation, and has increasingly connected it to regional and Pan-African priorities. The Tana Forum was established in 2012 by the Institute for Peace and Security Studies at Addis Ababa University, following the African Union’s 2009 Tripoli Declaration calling for African-led approaches to peace and security. It serves as an informal diplomatic and policy platform bringing together current and former African leaders, regional organisations, researchers and civil society representatives. The 2026 gathering is expected to involve around 250 participants and is examining economic sovereignty, resource governance, regional integration, institutional capacity and the effects of geopolitical competition on African security. Unlike formal African Union summits, the Tana Forum does not negotiate binding agreements. Its discussions are intended to inform policy debates and decision-making within African institutions. Share: More News Zamzam Bank Posts 1.8 Billion Birr Pre-Tax Profit as Deposits Hit 144% of Target Net profit tops 1 billion Birr for the first time, customer base grows 144 percent to over 973,000, digital users rise more than 150 percent, and capi Read More India’s Kilitch Drugs to Sell Ethiopian Pharmaceutical Plant for $14 Million State-owned HarmeeCare Pharmaceuticals to acquire antibiotic manufacturing facility as Indian drugmaker redirects investment toward domestic expansion Read More Dangote Refinery Opens New Investment Route Through Botswana Access Bank targets up to US dollars 500 million through Botswana-listed securities. Access Bank Botswana has opened a new investment channel for Afri Read More Ethiopia Elevates Sea Access to Existential Security Issue at Tana Forum Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed links Ethiopia’s dependence on foreign ports to national sovereignty, regional security and trade competitiveness as Afric Read More Menu News Feature Insights Interview Startups Editorial About us 