      "content": "Kindiki arrives in Ethiopia for African security forum For You News Faida Cafe Shows Radio TV Podcasts Live Events Search Kindiki arrives in Ethiopia for African security forum By Citizen Reporter October 24, 2026 11:55 (EAT) Add as a Preferred Source on Google Follow us Follow on Whatsapp Follow on Google Follow on Twitter Kenya's Deputy President Kithure Kindiki is received by Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed on Satruday, October 24, 2026 for the 12th TANA High Level Forum on security in Africa, Bahir Dar, Ethiopia. /X/KITHURE KINDIKI Audio By Vocalize Deputy President Kithure Kindiki has arrived in Bahir Dar, Ethiopia, to attend the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa. Kindiki was received at Bahir Dar International Airport on Saturday by Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed. The forum, running from October 23 to 25, brings together African leaders, policymakers and other stakeholders to discuss the continent’s peace and security challenges under the theme, “Pathways to Peace in Africa: Economic Sovereignty and Geopolitical Transformation.” Discussions will examine how Africa’s management of its resources, economic institutions and regional integration can contribute to lasting peace. According to the organisers, the agenda includes resource wealth and monetary sovereignty for industrial development, macroeconomic governance, and the impact of illicit economies on regional stability. About 250 participants are expected, including sitting and former heads of state and government, diplomats, academics, civil society representatives and private sector leaders. Organised by the Institute for Peace and Security Studies at Addis Ababa University, the Tana Forum provides an informal platform for leaders and experts to debate African-led responses to security challenges. It was established in 2012 following the African Union’s 2009 Tripoli Declaration, which called for African-led approaches to peace and security. trending now EACC freezes Ksh.245M assets linked to Lands ministry officer 19 hours ago 22 killed, several injured in bus-lorry collision along Nairobi-Mombasa highway Two-year manhunt ends as Kenyan man arrested in Tanzania over wife's 2024 murder 20 hours ago Police inspector Sang arrested over lawyer Kyalo Mbobu, Dr. Victoria Mutiso murders Collapsed Eastleigh building had no construction approval, NEMA licence 21 hours ago latest stories Tags Kithure Kindiki Ethiopia peace forum Have a story? Tell us! Citizen Digital wants to hear from you. Send us your stories, videos, and photos. WhatsApp Web Submit Email Us SMS: 25170 Join the Discussion Share your perspective with the Citizen Digital community. Moderation applies Sign In to Publish No comments yet This discussion is waiting for your voice. Be the first to share your thoughts! about us Advertise With Us Privacy Policy Careers Contact Us Terms and Conditions contact info +254 719 060 000 digitalsales@royalmedia.co.ke Maalim Juma Road Off Dennis Pritt Road our Products Citizen Digital Viusasa ViuTravel Skiza Matangazo © 2026 Citizen Digital. All rights reserved. Unknown Title Unknown Artist • 0:00 / 0:00 Up Next",
      "summary": "Kenya's Deputy President Kithure Kindiki arrived in Bahir Dar, Ethiopia, on October 24, 2026, to participate in the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa. He was welcomed at the Bahir Dar International Airport by Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed. The forum, taking place from October 23 to 25, gathers African leaders, policymakers, and stakeholders to address the continent's peace and security issues under the theme “Pathways to Peace in Africa: Economic Sovereignty and Geopolitical Transformation.” Key discussions will focus on how Africa can leverage its resource management, economic institutions, and regional integration to foster lasting peace. The agenda includes topics such as resource wealth, monetary sovereignty for industrial development, macroeconomic governance, and the effects of illicit economies on regional stability. Approximately 250 participants are expected, including current and former heads of state, diplomats, academics, civil society representatives, and private sector leaders. The Tana Forum, established in 2012, serves as a platform for African-led security solutions.",
