      "content": "Somalia Humanitarian Aid Down 70% in Two Years, SoDMA Chief Warns | Dawan Africa | Dawan Africa Sunday, October 25, 2026 Search Home Politics Business Opinion Sports Explainers Cryptocurrency Culture & Society Features News Tech Podcasts Videos Blockchain Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Loading article content... Uncovering the Continent — Through Its Own Lens 21 October Street, 405 Suldan Business Park, Mogadishu, Somalia +252628881171 Info@dawan.africa Quick Links Home Latest News About Us Categories Culture & Society Features Cryptocurrency Tech Blockchain Blockchain Recent Posts Suspect Nabbed for Spending Sh6.7M Mistakenly Credited to His Account Oct 25, 2026 PS Omollo Asks Sugar Farmers to Repay Ruto’s Reforms With 2027 Votes Oct 25, 2026 Somalia Humanitarian Aid Down 70% in Two Years, SoDMA Chief Warns Oct 25, 2026 Stay Updated with African News Get the latest breaking news and analysis delivered to your inbox. Subscribe Join our community of informed readers. Unsubscribe anytime. © 2026 Dawan Africa. All rights reserved. Privacy Policy Terms of Service Search Somalia Humanitarian Aid Down 70% in Two Years, SoDMA Chief Warns October 25, 2026 1 min read HA By Hanad Abdi - Reporter MOGADISHU, 25 October 2026 Humanitarian aid to Somalia has fallen by 70% over the past two years and could stop entirely, according to Mahmoud Moallim Abdulle, commissioner of the Somali Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA). Moallim made the remarks on Sunday at a meeting in Mogadishu bringing together government officials and community representatives to discuss cooperation in responding to El Niño risks. “Humanitarian aid has declined by 70% over the past two years, and there are fears it could stop entirely,” he said. Related articles Suspect Nabbed for Spending Sh6.7M Mistakenly Credited to His Account PS Omollo Asks Sugar Farmers to Repay Ruto’s Reforms With 2027 Votes The commissioner said the decline in external support highlighted the need for the Somali government and public to strengthen self-reliance and contribute jointly to relief efforts.Organised by SoDMA and running on 25–26 October, the meeting brings together members of the diaspora, religious leaders, businesspeople and representatives of humanitarian organisations. Discussions focus on strengthening community solidarity, coordinating relief efforts and improving systems to prevent and respond to El Niño-related risks. Moallim said the two-day meeting would provide an opportunity to discuss how the government and different sections of society could work together on these priorities. More articles for you PS Raymond Omollo Advises ODM to Trust Ruto, Avoid Formal Pre-Election Pact Uganda Clears All 60 Contacts Linked to Kenya’s First Ebola Case Ad Ad Like ( 0 ) Save ( 0 ) Share More Articles Back to Top Related Articles Ruto in Mwingi Pushes Roads, Housing and Investment Drive Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → Mogadishu Power Firms Raise Tariffs by 5 Cents Amid Fuel Price Surge Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → Eritrea’s Information Minister Slams Ethiopia’s Red Sea Remarks at Tana Forum Oct 25, 2026 News Read more →",
      "summary": "Humanitarian aid to Somalia has plummeted by 70% over the last two years, raising concerns that it could cease entirely, according to Mahmoud Moallim Abdulle, the commissioner of the Somali Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA). Speaking at a meeting in Mogadishu, he emphasized the urgent need for the Somali government and citizens to enhance self-reliance and collaboratively contribute to relief efforts. The meeting, organized by SoDMA, aims to address the risks associated with El Niño and involves various stakeholders, including diaspora members, religious leaders, and humanitarian organizations. Discussions will focus on fostering community solidarity, coordinating relief efforts, and improving systems to mitigate El Niño-related challenges. Moallim highlighted that the two-day event serves as a platform for exploring how different sectors of society can work together to tackle these pressing issues. The situation underscores the critical need for sustained support and proactive measures to address the humanitarian crisis in Somalia.",
